From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cntlr: offer a renew to a node that has never configured itself

An agent is supposed to ask for its configuration with an
AP-Autoconfiguration Search, and ours do. A foreign one may simply not.

Measured 2026-09-21. A MediaTek agent joined our backhaul over 6 GHz, was
placed in the topology at depth 1 with its parent derived from the
association, and answered Topology Query 45 times in a minute - a healthy
member of the mesh by every reading we had. It kept its own SSID.

60 s of 1905 on the interface it joined through, by CMDU type:

    0002/0003  90/90  Topology Query / Response
    000d/000e  35/17  Higher Layer Query / Response  (none of the responses
                      were its own - it never answers those)
    0007       11     AP-Autoconfiguration Search: 6 from our agent,
                      5 from the controller, NONE from it
    0008/0009   0/0   no Response, no M2 - because nobody had asked
    000a        0     and no Renew either

So it never asked, and we never offered. It then sat in the mesh with a
foreign SSID: one network on paper, two in the air.

We do send Renew, but only to nodes that have already been configured once.
Both paths start from node->last_config being valid - cntlr_check_config_diff()
on a credential change, and the chain in cntlr_renew_nodes_configuration()
comparing last_config against cfg.last_change. A node that has never been
configured has no last_config, so it falls through every arm. The node that
needs a renew most is the only one that cannot get one.

Renew is the standard way of saying "ask me again", so offer it to a node
that is alive on 1905 and has never been configured, with a holdoff so an
agent that ignores it costs one multicast a minute and nothing more. Our own
nodes pass through this state only for the seconds before their own Search
lands.

Placed before the chain and not as an arm of it, for the same reason the
backhaul STA-MLD re-ask is: every arm there requires the timestamp this case
is defined by not having, and the first arm leaves the loop outright.

⚠️ Not yet shown to make the foreign agent accept the configuration - it is
not known whether it acts on a Renew it did not ask for. What is shown is
that nobody was offering one.
diff -u -r a/src/cntlr.c b/src/cntlr.c
--- a/src/cntlr.c
+++ b/src/cntlr.c
@@ -2051,6 +2051,42 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+#define UNCONFIGURED_RENEW_HOLDOFF 60	/* seconds between offers, per node */
+
+/* A node that has NEVER been configured falls through every arm below: each
+ * of them starts from node->last_config being valid, so they only refresh a
+ * node that configured itself once already.
+ *
+ * An agent is supposed to ask for its configuration - AP-Autoconfiguration
+ * Search - and ours do. A foreign one may simply not. Measured 2026-09-21 on
+ * a MediaTek agent that had joined our backhaul and was answering Topology
+ * Query normally: 60 s of 1905 on the interface it joined through carried 11
+ * Searches, six from our agent and five from the controller, and none of its
+ * own. It never received an M2, kept its own SSID, and the mesh was one
+ * network only on paper.
+ *
+ * Renew is the standard way of saying "ask me again", so offer it to a node
+ * that is alive on 1905 and has never been configured. Our own nodes pass
+ * through this state for the seconds before their own Search lands; a node
+ * that ignores the offer costs one multicast per holdoff and nothing else.
+ */
+static bool cntlr_unconfigured_renew_due(struct controller *c, struct node *n)
+{
+	if (!timestamp_invalid(&n->last_config))
+		return false;
+
+	/* Silent on 1905 is not "unconfigured", it is "not there yet". */
+	if (timestamp_invalid(&n->last_cmdu))
+		return false;
+
+	if (c->uptime < n->unconfigured_renew_at)
+		return false;
+
+	n->unconfigured_renew_at = c->uptime + UNCONFIGURED_RENEW_HOLDOFF;
+
+	return true;
+}
+
 static void cntlr_renew_nodes_configuration(struct controller *c)
 {
 	trace("%s: --->\n", __func__);
@@ -2067,6 +2103,25 @@
 	/* When at least one running node was configured with older config, */
 	/* send autoconfig renew */
 	list_for_each_entry(node, &c->agentlist, list) {
+		/* Deliberately before the chain below and not an arm of it:
+		 * every arm there requires last_config to be valid, which is
+		 * exactly what a never-configured node does not have. Asking
+		 * from inside the chain would skip the one node that needs it.
+		 */
+		if (cntlr_unconfigured_renew_due(c, node)) {
+			uint8_t renew_addr[6] = {0x01, 0x80, 0xc2, 0x00, 0x00, 0x13};
+			struct cmdu_buff *cmdu =
+				cntlr_gen_ap_autoconfig_renew(c, renew_addr);
+
+			info("%s: " MACFMT " has never configured itself - offering a renew\n",
+			     __func__, MAC2STR(node->almacaddr));
+
+			if (cmdu) {
+				send_cmdu(c, cmdu);
+				cmdu_free(cmdu);
+			}
+		}
+
 #if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
 		/* Deliberately before the chain below and not an arm of it: a
 		 * TTLM policy is unicast, carries no MLD configuration, and
diff -u -r a/src/cntlr.h b/src/cntlr.h
--- a/src/cntlr.h
+++ b/src/cntlr.h
@@ -229,6 +229,11 @@
 	uint64_t bstamld_requery_at;
 #endif
 
+	/* Controller uptime (s) before which this node is not offered an
+	 * unsolicited AP-Autoconfiguration Renew again. Zero means "at the
+	 * next opportunity". See cntlr_unconfigured_renew_due(). */
+	uint64_t unconfigured_renew_at;
+
 	/* CLOCK_MONOTONIC seconds since the tree first failed to place this
 	 * node, 0 while it is placed (topology.c). APPEND ONLY - see the
 	 * note above. */
